  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/support/stored_graph.rs` `impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[node_content]` is a fenced commentary citation on an explicit function with a body. That is a permitted form. The commentary gives a substantive reason why the function's zero execution is acceptable. `Domain` requires the `node_content` callback. This storage-only fixture delegates `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` to `FileEvidenceStorage` using only `node_path`, and its `NodeContent` is the uninhabited `UnreadContents`. So the function cannot return a value, and an intentional `panic!` guard enforces the no-content-read invariant. The explanation agrees with the current source. I also checked how the fixture is used. `tests/storage.rs` uses `StoredGraph` for storage delegation and verification, and `tests/outline_content.rs` uses it only as the `ValidationCache` type parameter. Neither use contradicts the stated reliance. The function has no {impl} or {test} reference to the same item, so the citation is not redundant. I found no gap.",
